# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/54521
-All/GetDisplayMediaHiDpiBrowserTest.Capture/640x480

# Chromium test: ApplyDefaultSearchChangeForTesting(FROM_EXTENSION) on Linux
# leaves default_search_provider_ null (extension DSEs unsupported on Linux)
# while default_search_provider_source_ stays FROM_EXTENSION. Brave's
# DefaultSearchManager override causes MaybeShowDialog() to record the
# histogram twice with differing conditions (kControlledByPolicy and
# kExtensionControlled), failing the ExpectUniqueSample assertion.
# Already disabled on Windows in upstream source; upstream TODO crbug.com/429600559
# acknowledges the broken extension DSE test setup.
# Stable upstream: 0.3% flake rate over 30 days per LUCI Analysis.
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/54542
-SearchEngineChoiceDialogBrowserTest.DialogDoesNotShowWithExtensionEnabledThatOverridesDSE

# Upstream Chromium test (chrome/browser/webapps/installable/
# installable_manager_browsertest.cc). It asserts on InstallableManager's
# private task queue via friend access, and that assertion is racy whenever a
# second, unrelated GetData() request is in flight.
#
# InstallableTask::IncrementStateAndWorkOnNextTask() calls
# manager_->OnTaskFinished() (which POSTs FinishAndStartNextTask) and only then
# RunCallback(); CallbackTester::OnDidFinishInstallableCheck in turn POSTs the
# run loop's quit closure. So the pop-the-queue task is always ordered ahead of
# the quit closure. Separately, every primary page carrying a manifest reaches
# ManifestUpdateManager::OnManifestSeenOnPrimaryPage, which unconditionally
# schedules a ManifestSilentUpdateCommand; that command calls
# WebAppDataRetriever::CheckInstallabilityAndRetrieveManifest, i.e. a second
# InstallableManager::GetData(). When that request is queued while the test's
# own task is still running, FinishAndStartNextTask pops the test's task and
# immediately Start()s the background one, which completes off already-fetched
# page data and posts its own FinishAndStartNextTask. That post is still
# pending when the quit closure runs, so task_queue_.HasCurrent() is true at
# the assertion. Whether the background request lands inside that window is
# pure timing, hence the intermittency.
#
# Not caused by Brave. Brave has no chromium_src override or patch under
# components/webapps/browser/installable/, none under
# chrome/browser/web_applications/ outside os_integration/mac/ (macOS app
# shims), and none on the test itself. The only Brave override anywhere near
# this path is chromium_src/components/webapps/browser/features.cc disabling
# kWebAppsEnableMLModelForPromotion, and it is inert here: on this path that
# flag is read only by MLInstallabilityPromoter, and the fixture sets
# test::g_disable_banner_triggering_for_testing, which makes
# MLInstallabilityPromoter::StartPipeline() bail out before it starts any of
# its tasks.
#
# tools/chromium_tests_analysis/check-upstream-flake.py reports "STABLE UPSTREAM" (0.8% over 60 days),
# i.e. below the 1% bar, so this entry rests on the mechanism above and on
# Brave being provably not at fault, not on the flake rate. The rate only
# decides which file the entry belongs in: over that window linux-rel and
# win-rel both sit at ~0.2%, the Linux ASAN/LSAN builders show no failures at
# all, and macOS shows 1 flake in 10922 verdicts. The per-OS Ubuntu-22.04
# figure of 1.13% is dominated by ChromeOS builders, which Brave does not ship.
#
# So not browser_tests-linux-asan.filter: the race needs no sanitizer to occur,
# and upstream's own Linux ASAN/LSAN builders are the clean ones, so filtering
# only ASAN would leave the non-sanitizer Linux builds exposed. And not the
# all-platform browser_tests.filter: macOS shows no evidence of the failure, so
# there is no reason to drop coverage there, and Windows is already covered in
# browser_tests-windows.filter.
#
# Fixing this requires reordering the callback and the queue pop in upstream
# test or production code. A previous attempt, brave/brave-core#34786, patched
# the upstream test to add content::RunAllTasksUntilIdle() before the
# assertion; it was closed unmerged with no review comments. Independent of why
# it was closed, patching upstream test files is disallowed (TUF-001), and
# draining the run loop is a timing adjustment rather than synchronization.
#
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/58061 (Linux)
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/53717 (Windows, closed)
-InstallableManagerBrowserTest.CheckWebapp

# Upstream Chromium test (chrome/browser/ui/webui/tab_search/
# tab_search_page_handler_browsertest.cc). Fails intermittently on Linux x64
# with "Mock function called more times than expected ... TabUpdated ...
# over-saturated and active".
#
# The test sets EXPECT_CALL(page_, TabUpdated(_)).Times(0) on a StrictMock,
# which shadows the AnyNumber() expectation SetUpOnMainThread() installed, and
# then calls AddTabWithTitle() twice. Each of those spins the run loop
# (WaitForLoadStop(), ExecJs(), TitleWatcher::WaitAndGetTitle()), so any
# tab-UI update still pending for a tab in a browser the handler tracks gets
# delivered inside the test body and over-saturates the expectation. The most
# likely source is BrowserUiController::ScheduleUIUpdate(), which coalesces
# WebContents invalidations into a 200ms delayed ProcessPendingUIUpdates()
# that notifies TabChangeType::kAll, which BuildTabFieldMask() maps onto the
# fields HasTabSiteDataChanged() tests, so
# TabSearchPageHandler::OnTabDataChanged() forwards it as TabUpdated() with no
# debounce. There are other kAll producers and the CI log does not identify
# which one fired, but either way the test never bounds that window, so it is
# timing-dependent by construction.
#
# Upstream considers this fixture flaky too. At our pinned Chromium
# (153.0.8010.28), TabSearchPageHandlerTest.TabUpdated is disabled on all of
# Linux and .TabSearchUsedPref on Linux MSan, both citing crbug.com/537538766;
# .GetTabs is disabled on Linux dbg/asan/msan with no bug referenced; and
# TabsNotChanged itself is already disabled on ChromeOS (crbug.com/537468010).
#
# Brave is not the cause. Every Brave entry point into this mechanism was
# checked:
#  - The only Brave code that invalidates tab UI at all is Tor's:
#    tor_profile_manager.cc passes INVALIDATE_TYPE_ALL from
#    OnTorCircuitEstablished() on a Tor window's NTP tab, and
#    INVALIDATE_TYPE_URL from that same file's OpenURL() and from
#    onion_location_tab_helper.cc. ScheduleUIUpdate() handles the URL-only
#    invalidations synchronously and strips the flag before anything is
#    queued for the coalesced kAll path, and the ALL site is not reachable
#    for an about:blank tab in a non-Tor profile. Nothing else under brave/
#    calls NavigationStateChanged(), UpdateWebContentsStateAt(),
#    NotifyTabChanged(), TabChangeType::kAll or SetNeedsAttention(), and
#    Brave has no patch or chromium_src override on tab_ui_helper.* or the
#    tab alert controller, which are what TabData::FromTabInterface() reads
#    and whose inequality gates the kAll notify.
#  - The patch on browser_ui_controller.cc injects
#    MaybeUpdateSidebarForUrlChange() as the first statement of
#    ScheduleUIUpdate(), but it returns early unless INVALIDATE_TYPE_URL is
#    set for the active tab and its only effect is
#    Sidebar::UpdateSidebarItemsState(), a views-only refresh. It schedules no
#    task and touches no tab state.
#  - The patch on tab_model.cc swaps in BraveTabFeatures for every tab, which
#    attaches per-tab page-action controllers and WebContents observers (AI
#    Chat, PSST, containers, Speedreader, Wayback Machine, Playlist, Tor,
#    Brave News) plus a contextual side panel registration. None of them
#    reach the entry points above.
#  - The tab_search_page_handler and tab_search_ui chromium_src overrides only
#    add tab-organization and content-search mojo methods, a pref observer
#    emitting TabFocusEnabledChanged()/TabFocusShowFREChanged(), and extra
#    WebUI data source values. tab_data.cc's hooks are inert for an
#    about:blank tab, tab_converters.cc only redefines the TREE_NODE/SPLIT
#    collection case rather than BuildTabFieldMask(), and the existing patch
#    on this test file only adds two MOCK_METHODs.
#
# The only tab in a tracked browser here is InProcessBrowserTest's about:blank
# tab in browser1 (browser2 is tracked too, but this test never gives it a
# tab).
#
# script/check-upstream-flake.py reports "STABLE UPSTREAM" (0.2% over 30
# days), below the 1% bar, so this entry rests on the mechanism above and on
# Brave being ruled out rather than on the aggregate rate. That aggregate is
# diluted by two high-volume CQ trybots, linux-rel (~29k verdicts) and win-rel
# (~28k), together ~66% of all verdicts, whose events are all "failed" and
# none "flaky", i.e. attributable to the CL under test. Counting only flaky
# verdicts, which are the test's own fault, gives 67 on Linux --
# concentrated in linux_chromium_asan_rel_ng 5.48%, Linux ASan LSan Tests (1)
# 4.02%, linux_chromium_dbg_ng 2.41%, linux-wayland-mutter-rel-tests 1.88%,
# Linux Tests (dbg)(1) 1.03% and linux-arm64-wayland-rel-fyi 0.93% -- against
# 4 across every Windows and macOS builder combined. That gradient tracks how
# loaded the builder is, which is what a 200ms coalescing window predicts.
#
# Hence this file and not the all-platform browser_tests.filter: both Brave
# reports (browser linux x64 beta v1.95.92, origin linux x64 nightly v1.96.30)
# are Linux x64. And not browser_tests-linux-asan.filter: upstream's
# non-sanitizer Linux builders flake too, and Brave's own failures are Release
# builds.
#
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/58475
-TabSearchPageHandlerTest.TabsNotChanged

